From 9477e7a3843a17cb047b03facebb332a3e3405d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Martin Leitner-Ankerl <martin.ankerl@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:11:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] x86/cpu: Use prefetcht0 for prefetch() on 64-bit

x86-64 does not define ARCH_HAS_PREFETCH, so prefetch() from
<linux/prefetch.h> is just __builtin_prefetch(). The kernel builds with
-mno-sse, and clang then drops __builtin_prefetch() without any warning,
while gcc emits prefetcht0. So in a clang-built x86-64 kernel every
prefetch() is gone. An x86-64 defconfig vmlinux has 8 prefetcht0 with
clang 22.1.8, all of them from inline assembly, and 62 with gcc 16.2.1.

Give x86-64 its own prefetch(): a prefetcht0 in inline assembly. Every
x86-64 CPU has that instruction, so nothing is patched at boot. The
32-bit prefetch() does not fit: it is an alternative that starts as no
instruction and becomes prefetchnta on CPUs with SSE. prefetchnta is a
non-temporal prefetch, for data that is read once, so gcc builds, which
emit prefetcht0 today, would change. With this patch they keep their
prefetcht0, and clang builds get the same. ARCH_HAS_PREFETCH, which
tells <linux/prefetch.h> that the architecture has its own prefetch(),
is now defined for 32-bit and 64-bit, like ARCH_HAS_PREFETCHW already
is.

With this, clang's vmlinux has 57 prefetcht0 and gcc's still 62. The 49
new ones are in 34 functions, e.g. __blk_mq_alloc_requests(),
blk_mq_end_request_batch(), prepend_path(), task_sched_runtime(),
__alloc_skb(), net_rx_action(), tcp_ack() and the receive paths of
e1000, tg3, sky2, r8169 and forcedeth.

This makes polled io_uring reads from null_blk about 2% faster, and
getcwd() 40 directories deep about 1%. Measured in a KVM guest with one
CPU, pinned to one core of a Ryzen 9 7950X at a fixed 4.5 GHz, clang
22.1.8, 6 boots of each kernel in turns. ns per call, median over the
boots and the range:

                    without this patch     with this patch
  io_uring IOPOLL   680.1 (679.6..684.6)   664.9 (663.1..668.4)   -2.3%
  getcwd()          912.9 (912.0..1019.4)  901.4 (900.1..907.3)   -1.3%

io_uring IOPOLL reads 4 KiB at a time with a poll queue, 32 reads per
submit. As a control, the same benchmark built with gcc, where both
kernels have the same 62 prefetcht0, differs by +0.4% and 0.0%.
pread() with O_DIRECT from null_blk, UDP and TCP over loopback, times()
and clock_gettime(CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID) don't change more than the
two gcc kernels do. Unfortunately the NIC receive paths need real
hardware, so they are not measured.

Signed-off-by: Martin Leitner-Ankerl <martin.ankerl@gmail.com>
---
 arch/x86/include/asm/processor.h | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/x86/include/asm/processor.h b/arch/x86/include/asm/processor.h
index 77488ff5eb8c..64748b8705e3 100644
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/processor.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/processor.h
@@ -624,15 +624,25 @@ extern int			bootloader_version;
 extern char			ignore_fpu_irq;
 
 #define HAVE_ARCH_PICK_MMAP_LAYOUT 1
+#define ARCH_HAS_PREFETCH
 #define ARCH_HAS_PREFETCHW
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_X86_32
 # define BASE_PREFETCH		""
-# define ARCH_HAS_PREFETCH
 #else
 # define BASE_PREFETCH		"prefetcht0 %1"
 #endif
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_X86_64
+/*
+ * Every x86-64 CPU has prefetcht0. Not __builtin_prefetch(): the kernel
+ * builds with -mno-sse, and clang then drops the builtin without a warning.
+ */
+static inline void prefetch(const void *x)
+{
+	asm("prefetcht0 %0" : : "m" (*(const char *)x));
+}
+#else
 /*
  * Prefetch instructions for Pentium III (+) and AMD Athlon (+)
  *
@@ -645,6 +655,7 @@ static inline void prefetch(const void *x)
 			  X86_FEATURE_XMM,
 			  "m" (*(const char *)x));
 }
+#endif /* CONFIG_X86_64 */
 
 /*
  * 3dnow prefetch to get an exclusive cache line.
-- 
2.56.0

